index 0000000..8bd93a2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,190 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots aren't in Rails Campfire as this app ports it. Any member can set them up. Administrators
+manage everyone's.
+
+## Data
+
+- A bot is a `users` row with `role = 2` (`Role::Bot`), so it joins rooms, has memberships and
+  authors messages like a person. Unlike a person's, `User::create` doesn't grant it the open
+  rooms: see "Rooms" below.
+- Its `bots` row (`campfire_db::Bot`, created by `schema::ADDITIONS`) holds `user_id` (unique),
+  `creator_id` and `token`. It's a separate table rather than columns on `users`:
+  `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` is idempotent on every boot, `users` keeps the Rails columns that
+  `columns_test` checks, and the Rails image ignores it.
+- The bot code is `"<user id>-<token>"` (Rails Campfire's `bot_key` format). The token has 24
+  alphanumeric characters. `Bot::authenticate` looks the bot up by id and compares tokens with
+  `rails_compat::secure_compare`.
+- `password_digest` is `NOT NULL`, so a bot gets the bcrypt digest of a random password. It has no
+  email address, so it can't sign in, and session transfers refuse bots.
+- Deleting a bot deletes its `bots` row (and its webhook) and deactivates its user, so its messages
+  keep their author. Resetting a code (`PATCH /bots/:bot_id/key`) replaces the token.
+- A bot's optional webhook is a `bot_webhooks` row (`campfire_db::Webhook`: `bot_id` unique, `url`),
+  another `schema::ADDITIONS` table: a column added to `bots` wouldn't reach databases that already
+  have the table. `Webhook::assign` strips the URL, deletes the row for a blank one, and otherwise
+  validates an absolute `http(s)://` URL with a host (error on `webhook_url`) and upserts.
+
+## Rooms
+
+- A bot is in the rooms people add it to (closed rooms' member pickers, direct rooms), and in
+  every open room when the form's "Add to all open rooms" switch (`bot[all_open_rooms]`, a
+  `check_box` with its hidden `"0"`, off by default) is on. A missing or blank key leaves the
+  setting as it is.
+- The switch is a `bots_in_all_open_rooms` row (`bot_id` unique), another `schema::ADDITIONS`
+  table. `Bot::update_all_open_rooms(true)` upserts it and, after commit, grants the bot every
+  open room (`room::grant_open_rooms_to`); `false` deletes it and leaves the memberships alone.
+  Deleting a bot deletes the row.
+- `Rooms::Open`'s grant to everyone (`room::grant_to_active_users`, on create and on becoming
+  open) takes the active people and only the bots with that row.
+- Every membership insert goes through `room::insert_memberships`, which `RETURNING "user_id"`s
+  the rows it actually created (`ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` skips existing members) and emits
+  `Event::BotJoined { room_id, user_id }` for the bots among them, unless the room is direct. So
+  `joined` fires exactly once per membership: re-saving a room or switching the setting on again
+  sends nothing, and a bot removed and added back joins again.
+
+## Routes
+
+`controllers::app_routes` holds routes Rails doesn't have. They're matched after the whole Rails
+table, which stays identical to `vectors/campfire_routes.json`. The route tests seed their corpus
+from both tables.
+
+| Route | Action |
+|---|---|
+| `GET /bots`, `GET /bots/new`, `POST /bots` | list (no pagination, `Cache-Control: no-store`), form, create |
+| `GET /bots/:id/edit`, `PATCH/PUT /bots/:id`, `DELETE /bots/:id` | edit, rename/webhook/avatar, delete |
+| `PATCH/PUT /bots/:bot_id/key` | new code |
+| `DELETE /bots/:bot_id/suspension` | reactivate a suspended bot (administrators only) |
+| `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages` | the bot API (`messages::by_bots`) |
+
+Bots someone can't manage answer 404 (`Bot::find_manageable_by`).
+
+## The bot API
+
+- The chain skips session authentication and forgery protection. `concerns::authenticate_bot`
+  returns 401, then `set_room` returns 404 (an unknown room, or one the bot isn't in). A blank body
+  without a multipart `attachment` gets 422. Success is `201` with `Location: /messages/:id`, a path.
+- The raw body is the rich-text body, as Rails Campfire's `request.body.read` is. HTML is sanitized
+  on render. Creating, broadcasting and push use the composer's `create_message` and
+  `broadcast_create`.
+- `curl -d` sends `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`. kit would reject bodies like `100% done` as
+  malformed params before routing. `by_bots::read_raw_body`, a middleware in `app::router`, parses
+  the body for this path and drops param errors.
+- `by_bots::filter_key` is the `PathFilter` that `KitConfig::filter_logged_path` and
+  `FrontConfig::filter_logged_path` use, so request logs show the code as `[FILTERED]`.
+
+## UI
+
+- The account page nav links to `/bots` ("Set up chat bots") for everyone. The golden pages
+  `account_edit_*.html` and `facts.json` (`bot.svg`) were updated for it.
+- Bot items label the edit link with `aria-label="Edit <name>"` and no screen-reader text. That
+  keeps the name the item's only text, so `getByText(name)` stays unambiguous. No other control's
+  accessible name contains "bot code".
+- The "Add to all open rooms" switch is a real checkbox inside its `<label>` (the visible text is
+  its accessible name). `switch__input--cover` stretches the checkbox, transparent, over the
+  styled `.switch`, so the box itself has a size and takes the clicks (the plain `switch__input`
+  is 0×0, which browser automation treats as hidden).
+- Bots are left out of the account's people list (`account_users`,
+  `User::active_without_bots_ordered`), and `accounts/users#update/destroy` return 404 for them, so
+  no role switch turns one into a person. They still appear wherever people are picked for rooms.
+
+## Bots per member
+
+- The account page's "Bots per member" (administrators only) is a number field,
+  `account[settings][bots_per_member]`, that submits on change through the existing settings form
+  (`PUT /account.<id>`). `AccountSettings::bots_per_member` reads it: 5
+  (`DEFAULT_BOTS_PER_MEMBER`) when it isn't stored, and never below 0. The setter casts as Active
+  Model's integer type (`ruby_compat::integer_cast`); blank or non-numeric removes the key.
+- The default is applied on read, not merged into the hash, so the settings JSON stays exactly what
+  Rails writes (`account_test` checks it) until an administrator sets the number.
+- `Bot::count_created_by` counts a creator's *active* bots (a deleted bot has no `bots` row and a
+  deactivated user). `Bot::limit_for` is `None` for administrators. `Bot::reached_limit` is
+  `Some(limit)` when the count is at or past the limit, so lowering the limit leaves existing bots
+  alone and only blocks new ones.
+- `Bot::create` checks inside the write transaction (the single writer serializes creates), and
+  fails with an error on `base` (`Errors::full_messages` shows `base` errors without an attribute
+  name): `POST /bots` re-renders the form with 422 and "You’ve reached the limit of N bots per
+  member." `GET /bots/new` at the limit redirects to `/bots`, and the list shows that message in
+  place of the "Add a chat bot" link.
+- The admin golden pages `account_edit_*.html` were updated by hand for the new field.
+
+## Webhooks
+
+- The form's "Webhook URL" field is `bot[webhook_url]`. Leaving the key out of a request leaves the
+  webhook as it is; an empty value removes it.
+- `messages#create` (the composer only, not the bot API, so bots answering bots can't loop) calls
+  `messages::bot_webhooks::deliver_later` after the broadcast. Everything else happens in a job on
+  the `JobKind::BotWebhook` queue (`Jobs::perform_later_on`), which has its own workers, so neither
+  the poster nor other jobs wait on a slow bot.
+- The job reads `message.mentionees` (room members), drops the author, and keeps the active bots
+  with a webhook (`Webhook::of_active_bots`). Each bot gets its own payload, posted concurrently:
+
+  ```json
+  { "event": "mention",
+    "author":  { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+    "room":    { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot key>/messages" },
+    "message": { "id": 118, "body": { "text": "…", "html": "<stored rich text>" }, "link": "/rooms/5/@118" } }
+  ```
+
+  `text` is `plain_text_body` with every `@<bot name>` removed, then stripped (as Rails Campfire's
+  `without_recipient_mentions`). `room.name` is the display name for the bot (direct rooms have
+  none of their own). Paths are relative.
+- `Event::BotJoined` is a job on the same `JobKind::BotWebhook` queue
+  (`bot_webhooks::register_jobs`). If the bot is active, has a webhook, and is still in the
+  (non-direct) room when the job runs, its webhook gets:
+
+  ```json
+  { "event": "joined",
+    "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot key>/messages" } }
+  ```
+
+  The answer is handled as a mention's. The event comes from the models (often from an
+  after-commit hook), not a request, so the answer's broadcast renders on
+  `page::DETACHED_BASE_URL` (`http://example.org`, `ApplicationController.renderer`'s default)
+  and its body is canonicalized without a request host; message partials don't use absolute URLs.
+- `integrations::webhooks::deliver` does the HTTP: no proxy, no private network guard (bots often
+  run on `localhost` or the LAN; the push and unfurl clients keep theirs), 7 s connect and read
+  timeouts, 15 s for the whole exchange, answers up to 1 MB.
+- Answers: `200` with `text/plain` (escaped, lines joined with `<br>`) or `text/html` (stored as rich
+  text, sanitized on render) is created as the bot's message, if it's still in the room, and
+  broadcast and pushed like any. `204`, other 2xx, other types or a blank body post nothing.
+  Failures (other statuses, timeouts, connection errors) are only logged.
+- Broadcasting outside a request uses `messages::broadcast_create_at` with the base URL and host
+  captured from the posting request.
+- Bots are `users` rows in the room, so the mention autocomplete (`autocompletable/users`) already
+  suggests them.
+
+## Misbehaving bots
+
+The limits are constants in `campfire_db::models::throttle` (no settings): `MAX_MESSAGES` (10) in
+`MESSAGES_WINDOW` (10 s), `COOLDOWN` (30 s), `MAX_COOLDOWN_ATTEMPTS` (5), `MAX_FAILED_DELIVERIES`
+(10).
+
+- State lives in two more `schema::ADDITIONS` tables, so it survives restarts and follows the
+  app's `Clock` (tests travel in time): `bot_throttles` (`campfire_db::Throttle`: `bot_id` unique,
+  `cooldown_ends_at`, `cooldown_attempts`, `failed_deliveries`) and `bot_suspensions`
+  (`campfire_db::Suspension`: `bot_id` unique, `reason` = `flooding` | `failing_webhook`).
+- Flooding counts the bot's own messages (`creator_id`, `created_at` newer than now − 10 s; the
+  added `index_messages_on_creator_id_and_created_at` keeps that cheap), so API posts and webhook
+  answers count alike without a counter. `Bot::admit_message` runs *inside the write transaction
+  that creates the message* (`messages::create_message_admitted`), so concurrent posts can't all
+  slip under the limit; a refusal returns `Ok(Err(admission))`, so the throttle's own writes
+  (starting a cooldown, counting an attempt, suspending) commit while no message is created.
+- The post over the limit starts the cooldown (`cooldown_attempts = 0`) and gets `429` with
+  `Retry-After: 30`. Every post during the cooldown, API or webhook answer, is an attempt: `429`
+  with the seconds left (rounded up), and the fifth suspends the bot (that fifth still answers
+  `429`; the next gets `403`). A cooldown after the previous one ended counts afresh.
+- Webhook answers refused by the throttle are dropped (logged at info). Webhooks are still
+  delivered during a cooldown; only suspension stops them.
+- `Answers::deliver` records every delivery (`Bot::record_delivery`): `WebhookError::is_failed_delivery`
+  is any error except `ReplyTooLarge` (that was a `2xx`). Success resets `failed_deliveries`; the
+  tenth failure in a row suspends.
+- Suspended: `messages::by_bots::ensure_not_suspended` answers `403` right after authentication
+  (before `set_room`); `Webhook::of_active_bots` leaves suspended bots out, so neither `mention`
+  nor `joined` reaches them. The user stays active, so rooms, messages, `Bot::count_created_by` and
+  the bots list are unchanged.
+- The bots list shows "Suspended" (a `<strong>`) and the reason
+  (`SuspensionReason::description`). Administrators get a "Reactivate" button (`button_to`
+  `DELETE /bots/:bot_id/suspension`, `bots/suspensions#destroy`, with `data-turbo-confirm`, which
+  Turbo shows with the native `confirm()`). Anyone else gets `403` there
+  (`ensure_can_administer`). `Bot::reactivate` deletes the suspension and the throttle row, so the
+  bot starts afresh; `Bot::destroy` does the same.
